  "dom_id": "step-q7235",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194531/assets/90626ef73fbd9c0bd94802275961ca0c604a76f0d01faa1889b7dd3524ad6132.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({4}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the graph of $y=k({4}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={4}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$",
